lessons.body:
CSS controls how a page looks. Spacing between lines helps readers track the text, and limiting the width of a column keeps lines from stretching across a large screen. A fixed-width column can force sideways scrolling on a phone. A maximum width lets the column shrink when there is less room.

Worked example (fictional): Add this input inside the `head` of your seed swap page, after the character encoding line. The viewport setting tells mobile browsers to lay out the page at the device’s width.
```html
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<style>
  body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #1a1a1a;
    background: #fff;
  }
  main {
    max-width: 65ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem;
  }
  a { color: #164a8b; text-decoration: underline; }
  a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #164a8b; outline-offset: 3px; }
</style>
```
Output: On a wide screen, the text sits in a centered column. In a narrow window, the lines wrap and padding keeps text away from the edges. Links stay underlined, so color is not the only clue. When you Tab to a link, a visible outline shows where keyboard focus is.

Practice: Temporarily change `max-width: 65ch` to `width: 900px`. Open a narrow browser window and compare the sideways scrolling with the original. Restore `max-width`, set browser zoom to 200%, and check that you can still read the event details without scrolling sideways. Use Tab to confirm that you can see which link is focused.

Suggested mentor discussion, if you request a mentor: Compare the page at two widths. At which width is the first paragraph easiest to read, and what makes the difference?
